  "id": "F1",
  "file": "src/git/repos.ts",
  "line": 758,
  "title": "Encoded credentials survive the new diagnostic's redaction",
  "detail": "diagnose() builds the refusal message from a path with redactCredentials (which matches registered credentials literally) and redactPrivate, then calls checkPublicationText(section, name(path), []) with no entries, which enforces only the decoding limit. A file whose name contains a percent-encoded (or otherwise encoded) registered credential, in a change that also exceeds the decoding limit, therefore yields a refusal message containing the recoverable credential, and that message is stored and shown. AGENTS.md: decode before you redact. Decode the path the way the publication check does before redacting credentials and private strings, and if a decoded form still matches a credential or private entry, withhold the filename (\"[redacted filename]\") rather than printing it. Add a test with a percent-encoded registered credential in a filename plus oversized content: the refusal names the commit and \"[redacted filename]\" and never contains the encoded value."
 },
 {
  "id": "F2",
  "file": "src/gates/audit.ts",
  "line": 425,
  "title": "Audit filenames are checked for private strings before they are normalized",
  "detail": "The finding's file is checked with privateMatches on the raw path and only then passed through redactGateOutput. A new path such as `.tmp-gate/secret-\\x1b[31mhost.example.txt` doesn't match the private entry `secret-host.example` while the escape sequence is in it; redactGateOutput then strips the escape and produces the literal private string, which reaches persisted audit findings and the implementer's next-round feedback. Canonicalize first (redactGateOutput / strip terminal sequences, redact credentials), then run privateMatches on the normalized filename and replace it with \"[redacted filename]\" on a match. Apply the same order to the detail text. Add a test with an escape sequence splitting a private entry in an added path."
 },
 {
  "id": "F3",
  "file": "src/git/repos.ts",
  "line": 766,
  "title": "Diagnosis is quadratic in the number of files",
  "detail": "For every patch section, diagnose() searches the whole raw entry list (`raw.find(ids)`), so a refused change with many files does O(files\u00b2) synchronous work after the aggregate check has already refused, outside git's subprocess timeout and without observing cancellation. Index the raw entries by blob id pair (a Map) so each section is a constant-time lookup, and keep diagnosis bounded: if it can't locate the offending file within a fixed budget, rethrow the original refusal unchanged."
 },
 {
  "id": "F4",
  "file": "src/gates/audit.ts",
  "line": 292,
  "title": "Legitimate dot-directory additions get scratch-output advice",
  "detail": "root-hidden-addition warns on any new file beneath a root dot-directory and tells the implementer to \"Remove accidental scratch output\". A required new file such as `.github/workflows/<name>.yml` or `.github/dependabot.yml` gets the same advice, which can lead an implementer to delete it. Exempt `.github/` (and any other dot-directory the repository already tracks files in at the base commit), so the rule targets new hidden directories like `.tmp-gate/`. Add a test that a new `.github/workflows/x.yml` gets no warning."
